 ### Call the next ticket
 
+Implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (mode `any`, `priority_only` or
+`normal_only`). Authorization is not part of the action yet; the HTTP/Livewire
+caller is responsible for it.
+
 ```text
 Attendant
    |
    v
-Authorized call action
+CallNextTicket (single DB transaction)
    |
    v
-Load waiting state for the queue
+Lock the queue row; re-read is_active (reject if inactive)
    |
    v
-Apply deterministic priority + age + fairness rules
+Apply mode filter + priority/age/id ordering + 3-ticket fairness (any mode)
    |
    v
-Atomically mark exactly one ticket as called
+Lock the candidate row; re-check it is still waiting (skip and retry if not)
    |
    v
-Return called ticket / display payload
+Mark it called (status, called_at, called_by_user_id) and return it
 ```
 
-The implementation must prevent two concurrent attendants from successfully
-calling the same waiting ticket. The exact locking strategy belongs to the
-implementation and database transaction layer; the domain invariant is that a
-ticket can be called only once.
-
-> TODO (follow-up from review): link the test covering the single-call
-> invariant and concurrent-call protection once implemented.
+Two concurrent attendants cannot both succeed for the same ticket: the queue
+row lock serializes call-next calls, and the candidate row lock with its
+`waiting` re-check covers `Ticket::call()`, which does not lock the queue.
+Covered by `tests/Feature/CallNextTicketTest.php` (ordering, modes, fairness,
+inactive queue, no-eligible-ticket, and the write-once result). The
+lock-and-recheck race path itself is not exercised by an automated test, because
+Pest runs on a single SQLite connection.
 
 ## Authorization boundary
 
